    "case_id": "733",
    "case_summary": "A 13-year-old female presented with vision loss and scotomas in the right eye. Five days prior, her classmate had directed an online-bought handheld high-intensity LED light (30 chip-on-board LEDs emitting 5 W, 100 lm/W at 500 mA) at her right eye multiple times at very short distance with flash mode. She sought immediate medical attention at a local hospital for blurred vision and scotomas but was referred elsewhere.\n\nOn initial presentation 5 days after exposure:- Best corrected visual acuity (BCVA): 6/20 right eye, 20/20 left eye- Anterior segment examination: unremarkable - Fundus examination: unremarkable\n\n- Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) at 3 days post-exposure:  Image Title: Initial OCT image  Image Description: Hyperreflective area at external retinal layers, disruption of ellipsoid zone, outer segment photoreceptor and interdigitation zone. Mild eminence of retinal pigment epithelium at fovea.- Fluorescein Angiography (FA) at 5 days post-exposure:  Image Title: FA image  Image Description: No leakage observed- Serial SD-OCT findings:  At 5 days: Mosaic-like hyporeflective outer retinal space with flat RPE  At 19 days: Partial restoration of ellipsoid zone, hyporeflective defect at interdigitation zone  At 33 days: Further restoration of ellipsoid zone and partial restoration of interdigitation zone  At 3 months: Normal external retinal layers- Multifocal Electroretinography (mfERG):  At 19 days: Decreased foveal peak P1 amplitudes in right eye  At 33 days: Improved foveal peak P1 amplitudes in right eye",
